<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  ICT3113 Assignment 1 — stress_ramp.jmx
  Owner: Part 1 — Yeo Kai Yuan (test harness).  Feeds slides 8 and 9.

  The stress test: one stepped, open-loop ramp on POST /tickets that keeps
  raising the ticket arrival rate until the system under test stops keeping up.
  The brief asks for one test that "determines a limit of the system under test";
  the limit this plan looks for is the highest arrival rate at which the baseline
  still answers within the response-time requirement and without errors.

  A STAIRCASE, NOT A SMOOTH RAMP, AND WHY.
  Each step holds a constant rate for ramp_step_duration_s seconds. A smooth
  linear ramp would never give the service a steady state at any particular rate,
  so a p95 computed over the ramp would not belong to any rate you could quote to
  a client. Flat steps mean each step yields its own p50/p95/p99, throughput and
  error rate, and the limit is then simply "the last step that still met the
  requirement". analysis/stress_summary.py slices the .jtl on the step boundaries
  documented below and in docs/playbooks/stress-test.md.

  Hand-written rather than GUI-generated so that it stays reviewable in a diff.
  See jmeter/README.md for the property list and the step-boundary table.
-->
<jmeterTestPlan version="1.2" properties="5.0" jmeter="5.6.3">
  <hashTree>
    <TestPlan guiclass="TestPlanGui" testclass="TestPlan" testname="ICT3113 A1 — stepped stress ramp on POST /tickets" enabled="true">
      <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">WHY THIS PLAN IS OPEN-LOOP, AND WHY A STRESS TEST ABSOLUTELY CANNOT BE CLOSED-LOOP.

This plan uses an Open Model Thread Group: an open-loop arrival-rate model in
which JMeter starts a thread for each scheduled arrival whether or not earlier
requests have been answered.

On a stress test the closed-loop failure is fatal, not merely inconvenient. With a
fixed pool of N threads the offered rate is N / response_time, so the harder the
service struggles the less work it is sent. The measured throughput then converges
on the service's own service rate by construction, the queue in front of Ollama
never grows, and the plan can never find a limit because it never exceeds one.
A closed-loop "stress test" reports that the system copes with everything you
offered it, because you only ever offered it what it could cope with.

Open-loop instead keeps offering the configured rate. When the rate passes what
the service can sustain, requests pile up: latency climbs step after step,
eventually the 180 s response timeout and the service's own 502s appear, and the
step at which that starts IS the limit we are looking for.

The assignment brief is explicit: "Traffic must be generated open-loop, at
controlled arrival rates: use the Open Model Thread Group or the Precise
Throughput Timer... Results from closed-loop tests will not be accepted as
evidence against throughput or latency requirements."

BASELINE NOTE. The plan drives the unmodified baseline. There is no concurrency
limit anywhere to protect it: the service does not bound its in-flight model
calls, so the queue forms inside Ollama. That is the behaviour we are here to
measure and record, not to fix.
# A2 candidate (service side, recorded here because this is the plan that exposes
# it): a bulkhead or semaphore around the model call, and backpressure - a 429 or
# a queue with a bounded wait - instead of unbounded queueing.</stringProp>
      <boolProp name="TestPlan.functional_mode">false</boolProp>
      <boolProp name="TestPlan.serialize_threadgroups">false</boolProp>
      <boolProp name="TestPlan.tearDown_on_shutdown">true</boolProp>
      <elementProp name="TestPlan.user_defined_variables" elementType="Arguments" guiclass="ArgumentsPanel" testclass="Arguments" testname="User Defined Variables" enabled="true">
        <collectionProp name="Arguments.arguments"/>
      </elementProp>
      <stringProp name="TestPlan.user_define_classpath"></stringProp>
    </TestPlan>
    <hashTree>
      <!--
        The stepped ramp. Six steps written out literally: JMeter cannot loop a
        schedule string, and the documented ${__groovy(...)} loop trick was
        rejected on purpose because it hides the actual rates from anyone reading
        the plan. Each step's rate is its own -J property, so a teammate can raise
        one step without touching the structure of the schedule.
      -->
      <org.apache.jmeter.threads.openmodel.OpenModelThreadGroup guiclass="org.apache.jmeter.threads.openmodel.gui.OpenModelThreadGroupGui" testclass="org.apache.jmeter.threads.openmodel.OpenModelThreadGroup" testname="Ticket intake (stepped ramp)" enabled="true">
        <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">STEP BOUNDARIES. With every property left at its default
(ramp_start_per_min=30, ramp_step_per_min=30, ramp_step_duration_s=120):

  step  offered rate   window (s from test start)   expected arrivals
  ----  ------------   -------------------------   -----------------
   1      30 / min          0 -  120                        60
   2      60 / min        120 -  240                       120
   3      90 / min        240 -  360                       180
   4     120 / min        360 -  480                       240
   5     150 / min        480 -  600                       300
   6     180 / min        600 -  720                       360
  drain   no arrivals     720 -  840                         0
  ----  ------------   -------------------------   -----------------
                                            total        1,260 tickets

Arrivals are a Poisson process, so the per-step counts vary a little around those
figures. The same table appears in jmeter/README.md and must be repeated in
docs/playbooks/stress-test.md, because analysis/stress_summary.py has to be TOLD
where the boundaries are: pass --step-seconds 120 (i.e. the same value as
-Jramp_step_duration_s) and it slices the .jtl into equal windows from the first
sample's timestamp.

If you change ramp_step_duration_s you must pass the same number to
stress_summary.py --step-seconds, or the reported per-step figures will be sliced
at the wrong places. That is the one coupling in this plan that is not enforced in
code, so it is written here, in the README and in the playbook.

HOW THE SIX RATES ARE SET. Each step reads its own property:
    -Jramp_rate_1 ... -Jramp_rate_6      (tickets per minute for that step)
If a step's property is not given, its default is computed from the shape
properties as  ramp_start_per_min + (step - 1) * ramp_step_per_min, which with the
defaults above gives 30, 60, 90, 120, 150, 180 per minute. So:
  * -Jramp_start_per_min=12 -Jramp_step_per_min=12  gives 12/24/36/48/60/72;
  * -Jramp_rate_6=240 raises only the top step and leaves the rest alone.

TODO(Part 4 — Toh Si Pei): the defaults above are a placeholder staircase, not a
workload figure. The rates that matter are the ones that bracket the throughput
requirement — you want steps below it, one at it, and steps above it, so that the
test actually demonstrates where the baseline stops meeting it. Choose them from
the workload model in ../workload/requirements.md and record them in the playbook.

THE DRAIN PAUSE AT THE END. An Open Model Thread Group interrupts its threads as
soon as the schedule ends, and an interrupted in-flight request is recorded as a
failed sample. At the top step a CPU-only model call can easily still be running
when the last step ends, so without a drain the plan would manufacture a burst of
errors that look like the limit we are hunting for. The trailing pause admits no
new arrivals but lets the requests already in flight finish and be recorded as the
latencies they really were. Its default is one step duration; override with
-Jramp_drain_s.

random_seed = ${__P(random_seed,0)} — a fresh arrival pattern per run, so the
spread across the three repeat runs includes arrival variability.</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="ThreadGroup.on_sample_error">continue</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="OpenModelThreadGroup.schedule">/* ICT3113 stepped stress ramp. ramp_steps=${__P(ramp_steps,6)} is recorded here so the run log states how many steps the operator intended; this schedule implements SIX steps LITERALLY, because JMeter cannot loop a schedule string. Changing ramp_steps does NOT change the schedule - add or remove a step line below by hand and update jmeter/README.md and the playbook. */
/* step 1 */ rate(${__P(ramp_rate_1,${__groovy((props['ramp_start_per_min'] ?: '30').toInteger())})}/min) random_arrivals(${__P(ramp_step_duration_s,120)} sec) rate(${__P(ramp_rate_1,${__groovy((props['ramp_start_per_min'] ?: '30').toInteger())})}/min)
/* step 2 */ rate(${__P(ramp_rate_2,${__groovy((props['ramp_start_per_min'] ?: '30').toInteger() + 1 * (props['ramp_step_per_min'] ?: '30').toInteger())})}/min) random_arrivals(${__P(ramp_step_duration_s,120)} sec) rate(${__P(ramp_rate_2,${__groovy((props['ramp_start_per_min'] ?: '30').toInteger() + 1 * (props['ramp_step_per_min'] ?: '30').toInteger())})}/min)
/* step 3 */ rate(${__P(ramp_rate_3,${__groovy((props['ramp_start_per_min'] ?: '30').toInteger() + 2 * (props['ramp_step_per_min'] ?: '30').toInteger())})}/min) random_arrivals(${__P(ramp_step_duration_s,120)} sec) rate(${__P(ramp_rate_3,${__groovy((props['ramp_start_per_min'] ?: '30').toInteger() + 2 * (props['ramp_step_per_min'] ?: '30').toInteger())})}/min)
/* step 4 */ rate(${__P(ramp_rate_4,${__groovy((props['ramp_start_per_min'] ?: '30').toInteger() + 3 * (props['ramp_step_per_min'] ?: '30').toInteger())})}/min) random_arrivals(${__P(ramp_step_duration_s,120)} sec) rate(${__P(ramp_rate_4,${__groovy((props['ramp_start_per_min'] ?: '30').toInteger() + 3 * (props['ramp_step_per_min'] ?: '30').toInteger())})}/min)
/* step 5 */ rate(${__P(ramp_rate_5,${__groovy((props['ramp_start_per_min'] ?: '30').toInteger() + 4 * (props['ramp_step_per_min'] ?: '30').toInteger())})}/min) random_arrivals(${__P(ramp_step_duration_s,120)} sec) rate(${__P(ramp_rate_5,${__groovy((props['ramp_start_per_min'] ?: '30').toInteger() + 4 * (props['ramp_step_per_min'] ?: '30').toInteger())})}/min)
/* step 6 */ rate(${__P(ramp_rate_6,${__groovy((props['ramp_start_per_min'] ?: '30').toInteger() + 5 * (props['ramp_step_per_min'] ?: '30').toInteger())})}/min) random_arrivals(${__P(ramp_step_duration_s,120)} sec) rate(${__P(ramp_rate_6,${__groovy((props['ramp_start_per_min'] ?: '30').toInteger() + 5 * (props['ramp_step_per_min'] ?: '30').toInteger())})}/min)
/* drain: no new arrivals, but requests already in flight are allowed to finish so that they are recorded as latencies rather than as interrupted samples */ pause(${__P(ramp_drain_s,${__groovy((props['ramp_step_duration_s'] ?: '120').toInteger())})} sec)</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="OpenModelThreadGroup.random_seed">${__P(random_seed,0)}</stringProp>
        <elementProp name="ThreadGroup.main_controller" elementType="org.apache.jmeter.threads.openmodel.OpenModelThreadGroupController"/>
      </org.apache.jmeter.threads.openmodel.OpenModelThreadGroup>
      <hashTree>
        <CSVDataSet guiclass="TestBeanGUI" testclass="CSVDataSet" testname="Ticket narratives (CSV Data Set Config)" enabled="true">
          <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">Identical configuration to the other two plans: columns
row_number,narrative,raw_label; quoted data because narratives contain commas and
newlines; header skipped and re-skipped on recycle; recycled at EOF; one shared
file handle.

Recycling matters more here than anywhere else. The full default ramp offers about
1,260 tickets and our team slice has 1,000 rows, so the file is re-read part-way
through. That is correct for a stress test: the question is how many tickets per
minute the service can absorb, not which tickets they were. It is also why
accuracy must never be computed from a stress run.

Defaults to the synthetic dev tickets. No team row reaches a model before the
golden set and the prediction record are committed and tagged.</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="filename">${__P(input_csv,../data/dev/synthetic_tickets.csv)}</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="fileEncoding">UTF-8</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="variableNames">row_number,narrative,raw_label</stringProp>
          <boolProp name="ignoreFirstLine">true</boolProp>
          <stringProp name="delimiter">,</stringProp>
          <boolProp name="quotedData">true</boolProp>
          <boolProp name="recycle">true</boolProp>
          <boolProp name="stopThread">false</boolProp>
          <stringProp name="shareMode">shareMode.all</stringProp>
        </CSVDataSet>
        <hashTree/>
        <HeaderManager guiclass="HeaderPanel" testclass="HeaderManager" testname="HTTP Header Manager — content type and reconciliation headers" enabled="true">
          <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">X-Request-ID is the join key between this .jtl and logs/service/*.jsonl, and it
is what makes a stress run auditable: when the service starts failing, the failed
samples can be matched to the log lines that explain why. X-Source-Row carries the
course CSV row number. Content-Type must be application/json for FastAPI.</stringProp>
          <collectionProp name="HeaderManager.headers">
            <elementProp name="Content-Type" elementType="Header">
              <stringProp name="Header.name">Content-Type</stringProp>
              <stringProp name="Header.value">application/json</stringProp>
            </elementProp>
            <elementProp name="X-Request-ID" elementType="Header">
              <stringProp name="Header.name">X-Request-ID</stringProp>
              <stringProp name="Header.value">${request_id}</stringProp>
            </elementProp>
            <elementProp name="X-Source-Row" elementType="Header">
              <stringProp name="Header.name">X-Source-Row</stringProp>
              <stringProp name="Header.value">${source_row}</stringProp>
            </elementProp>
          </collectionProp>
        </HeaderManager>
        <hashTree/>
        <HTTPSamplerProxy guiclass="HttpTestSampleGui" testclass="HTTPSamplerProxy" testname="POST /tickets" enabled="true">
          <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">The sampler name is the .jtl label column. It is deliberately the same string as
in the other two plans so that a stress step and a steady-load run at the same
rate are directly comparable. Do not rename it.

host has no usable default: the brief requires the load generator on a separate
machine, so the default is an invalid hostname that fails loudly rather than
quietly measuring a co-hosted run. Pass -Jhost.

response_timeout_ms = 180000 by default, longer than the service's
OLLAMA_TIMEOUT_S of 120 s. On a stress ramp this is the number that decides what
"failure" means: below it, a slow request is recorded as a real latency; at it,
the sample fails. Keeping it above the service's own timeout means the first
failures we see are the service's 502s - the system under test giving up - rather
than the load generator giving up first, which would tell us nothing about the SUT.
Raise it with care and record the value you used in the playbook: the limit this
plan finds depends on it.</stringProp>
          <elementProp name="HTTPsampler.Arguments" elementType="Arguments" guiclass="HTTPArgumentsPanel" testclass="Arguments" testname="Request body" enabled="true">
            <collectionProp name="Arguments.arguments">
              <elementProp name="" elementType="HTTPArgument">
                <boolProp name="HTTPArgument.always_encode">false</boolProp>
                <stringProp name="Argument.value">{"narrative": ${narrative_json}}</stringProp>
                <stringProp name="Argument.metadata">=</stringProp>
              </elementProp>
            </collectionProp>
          </elementProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.domain">${__P(host,SET-JHOST-TO-THE-SERVICE-HOST)}</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.port">${__P(port,8000)}</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.protocol">http</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.contentEncoding">UTF-8</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.path">/tickets</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.method">POST</stringProp>
          <boolProp name="HTTPSampler.postBodyRaw">true</boolProp>
          <boolProp name="HTTPSampler.follow_redirects">false</boolProp>
          <boolProp name="HTTPSampler.auto_redirects">false</boolProp>
          <boolProp name="HTTPSampler.use_keepalive">true</boolProp>
          <boolProp name="HTTPSampler.DO_MULTIPART_POST">false</boolProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.connect_timeout">${__P(connect_timeout_ms,10000)}</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.response_timeout">${__P(response_timeout_ms,180000)}</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.embedded_url_re"></stringProp>
        </HTTPSamplerProxy>
        <hashTree>
          <UserParameters guiclass="UserParametersGui" testclass="UserParameters" testname="Per-iteration ids (request_id, source_row)" enabled="true">
            <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">request_id = ${__UUID()} ; source_row = ${row_number}.

per_iteration is FALSE on purpose. With it true these values would be set from an
iterationStart listener, and JMeter notifies iteration listeners in reverse
registration order, so this element could fire before the CSV Data Set Config had
advanced to the current row; every source_row would then name the previous ticket,
and every reconciled row would be off by one with nothing in the .jtl to show it.
With it false the values are set from process(), immediately before the sampler,
once this iteration's CSV row is already bound.</stringProp>
            <collectionProp name="UserParameters.names">
              <stringProp name="37109963">request_id</stringProp>
              <stringProp name="-1111110730">source_row</stringProp>
            </collectionProp>
            <collectionProp name="UserParameters.thread_values">
              <collectionProp name="-1">
                <stringProp name="118040362">${__UUID()}</stringProp>
                <stringProp name="2006504376">${row_number}</stringProp>
              </collectionProp>
            </collectionProp>
            <boolProp name="UserParameters.per_iteration">false</boolProp>
          </UserParameters>
          <hashTree/>
          <JSR223PreProcessor guiclass="TestBeanGUI" testclass="JSR223PreProcessor" testname="JSON-escape the narrative (escaping, not logic)" enabled="true">
            <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">THIS IS ESCAPING, NOT LOGIC. JMeter has no JSON-escape function and our
narratives contain double quotes, newlines and non-ASCII punctuation, so the body
cannot be built by string concatenation. JsonOutput.toJson() returns the value
already wrapped in double quotes, which is why the body template is
{"narrative": ${narrative_json}} with no quotes of its own.

cacheKey makes Groovy compile the script once per run rather than once per sample.
That matters most in this plan: at the top step the generator is starting hundreds
of threads a minute, and a per-sample compile would spend load-generator CPU
inside the measured interval and inflate the latency we blame on the service.</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="cacheKey">ict3113-narrative-json-escape</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="filename"></stringProp>
            <stringProp name="parameters"></stringProp>
            <stringProp name="scriptLanguage">groovy</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="script">// Escaping only. See this element's comment.
// Names no Java type on purpose: JMeter 5.6.3's Groovy cannot compile one on a
// newer JDK and the failure is silent. See jmeter/README.md, "Groovy and the JDK
// version". JsonOutput is a Groovy class, which is safe.
vars.put('narrative_json', groovy.json.JsonOutput.toJson(vars.get('narrative')))</stringProp>
          </JSR223PreProcessor>
          <hashTree/>
          <ResponseAssertion guiclass="AssertionGui" testclass="ResponseAssertion" testname="HTTP 200" enabled="true">
            <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">Response code equals 200, and nothing else. This assertion is what defines the
error rate per step, and the error rate per step is how this plan finds the limit:
analysis/stress_summary.py compares each step's error rate against
--error-rate-limit and each step's p95 against --p95-limit-ms.

There is deliberately no assertion on the returned category. Checking it would be
measuring classification accuracy client-side inside a timed test, and would make
a correct-but-slow service indistinguishable from a fast-but-wrong one in the
error rate. Accuracy is measured only by scripts/run_accuracy.py against the
frozen golden set. A 200 carrying category = "UNPARSEABLE" counts as a success
here: the request was served.

Note for whoever reads the results: as the ramp bites, failures will arrive as a
mixture of the service's own 502s (the model timed out or errored) and JMeter's
"Non HTTP response code" entries (the 180 s response timeout, or a refused
connection once the service is overwhelmed). They mean different things. Split
them by responseCode before interpreting, and cross-check the 502s against the
error slug in logs/service/*.jsonl.</stringProp>
            <collectionProp name="Asserion.test_strings">
              <stringProp name="49586">200</stringProp>
            </collectionProp>
            <stringProp name="Assertion.test_field">Assertion.response_code</stringProp>
            <boolProp name="Assertion.assume_success">false</boolProp>
            <intProp name="Assertion.test_type">8</intProp>
            <stringProp name="Assertion.custom_message">Expected HTTP 200 from POST /tickets. A 502 is the service reporting a model error or timeout; a "Non HTTP response code" is the load generator's own timeout or a refused connection. Check logs/service/*.jsonl for this request_id.</stringProp>
          </ResponseAssertion>
          <hashTree/>
        </hashTree>
      </hashTree>
      <ResultCollector guiclass="SimpleDataWriter" testclass="ResultCollector" testname="Simple Data Writer (only when -Jjtl_out is set)" enabled="true">
        <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">scripts/run_load_test.sh writes results with JMeter's -l flag, whose own result
collector is configured from the jmeter.save.saveservice.* settings in
jmeter/user.properties. That file, not this element, is the authoritative save
configuration and is what appends the request_id and source_row columns.

This element only writes when -Jjtl_out=/absolute/path/results.jtl is given, so
the plan is also runnable by hand or from the GUI. Left unset it writes nothing.

error_logging=false: keep every sample. A stress run's successes are as important
as its failures — the per-step percentiles are computed from them.</stringProp>
        <boolProp name="ResultCollector.error_logging">false</boolProp>
        <objProp>
          <name>saveConfig</name>
          <value class="SampleSaveConfiguration">
            <time>true</time>
            <latency>true</latency>
            <timestamp>true</timestamp>
            <success>true</success>
            <label>true</label>
            <code>true</code>
            <message>true</message>
            <threadName>true</threadName>
            <dataType>true</dataType>
            <encoding>false</encoding>
            <assertions>true</assertions>
            <subresults>true</subresults>
            <responseData>false</responseData>
            <samplerData>false</samplerData>
            <xml>false</xml>
            <fieldNames>true</fieldNames>
            <responseHeaders>false</responseHeaders>
            <requestHeaders>false</requestHeaders>
            <responseDataOnError>false</responseDataOnError>
            <saveAssertionResultsFailureMessage>true</saveAssertionResultsFailureMessage>
            <assertionsResultsToSave>0</assertionsResultsToSave>
            <bytes>true</bytes>
            <sentBytes>true</sentBytes>
            <url>true</url>
            <threadCounts>true</threadCounts>
            <idleTime>true</idleTime>
            <connectTime>true</connectTime>
          </value>
        </objProp>
        <stringProp name="filename">${__P(jtl_out,)}</stringProp>
      </ResultCollector>
      <hashTree/>
    </hashTree>
  </hashTree>
</jmeterTestPlan>
